  number    =  1,
  pages     = "101--120",
  abstract  = "Abstract Since planners plan, they claim that their local,
               regional or national spatial plans are crucial for achieving
               spatial equity. Starting from the difficulties of defining
               spatial equity, the paper explores some European dimensions of
               spatial equity, thereby referring to three recent documents: the
               Commission's First Report on Social and Economic Cohesion, the
               Opinion on Spatial Planning of the Committee of the Regions and
               the draft European Spatial Development Perspective, having been
               approved by the Ministers of the Member States of the European
               Union in June 1997. This is followed by a brief assessment of the
               role of existing European networks of infrastructure, production,
               information and power, or those of memories and images, in
               creating spatial equity in Europe. The paper ends with a
               presentation and brief justification of the normative concept of
               the European Bunch of Grapes as a mental vision for spatial
               equity in Europe.",
  month     =  feb,
  year      =  1998,
  language  = "en"
}
